 "intro": {
  "text": "In the Ruy Lopez, or Spanish Game, White develops the bishop to b5 to put pressure on the knight that defends e5. White plays for quick development, castling and control of the centre, and would like to build a big pawn centre later with c3 and d4. The usual reply is 3...a6, the Morphy Defence, which asks the bishop to decide at once: take the knight or retreat.",
  "credit": {
   "source": "Wikibooks, Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6/3. Bb5",
   "url": "https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Chess_Opening_Theory/1._e4/1...e5/2._Nf3/2...Nc6/3._Bb5",
