     "notes": {
      "text": "The opening is a race to develop and control the centre. 1.e4 opens lines for your queen and king's bishop, controls d5 and f5, and keeps a Black pawn off e4 so your knight can later sit safely on f3. 1.d4 is the other main choice and gives slower, more closed games; knight moves to the edge like 1.Na3 do little for the centre.",
      "credit": {
       "source": "Wikibooks, Chess Opening Theory",
       "url": "https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Chess_Opening_Theory",
       "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
       "licenceUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
       "authors": "Wikibooks contributors (see the page history)"
      }
     }
