     "notes": {
      "text": "2...Nc6 defends e5 and controls d4. White usually develops the bishop next and keeps c3 and d4 in reserve. 3.Bb5, the Spanish, is the most popular and most studied move: by pressing the knight that guards e5 it threatens that pawn indirectly. 3.Bc4 (Italian) aims at f7 instead, and 3.d4 (Scotch) opens the centre at once.",
      "credit": {
       "source": "Wikibooks, Chess Opening Theory/1. e4/1...e5/2. Nf3/2...Nc6",
       "url": "https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Chess_Opening_Theory/1._e4/1...e5/2._Nf3/2...Nc6",
